.TH GIT-ISSUE 1 "2026-08-29" "git-issue" "Git Extensions"
.SH NAME
git-issue \- offline-first issue tracker stored in the git object store
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git issue
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B git issue
.RI [ id " [" comment ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git issue
tracks issues inside the repository itself. Issues, comments and their full
edit history are stored as git objects under dedicated refs, alongside the
code, and sync the way code does: fetch and push, just against different refs.
There is no server and no database.
.PP
With no arguments it lists issues; with an id it shows that issue. Those are
shorthands for
.B list
and
.BR show .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B add
File a new issue. Without
.B -t
an editor opens, as
.B git commit
does without
.BR -m .
.TP
.B list
List issues, with filters for state, type, label, assignee and author. On a
terminal the listing is nested under each issue's parent; down a pipe it prints
flat.
.B --tree
and
.B --no-tree
force it either way. An id lists the whole subtree filed under that issue.
.TP
.B show
Show one issue in full, or one comment on it and the replies under it.
.B --web
opens the issue's page on the tracker it was pulled from or pushed to, through
.BR git-web--browse (1).
.TP
.B comment
Post a comment on an issue.
.B --in-reply-to
nests it under another entry instead of starting a new thread.
.TP
.B edit
Edit an issue, or one comment on it. Each field that changed is written as its
own event, so concurrent edits to different fields both survive a merge.
.B --status
sets a raw status a bridge uses;
.B --status-reason
the raw close reason.
.TP
.B close
Close an issue \(em a single
.B status
event.
.B --as
records why:
.BR completed ", " not-planned " or " duplicate .
Naming a second issue with
.B --as duplicate
also writes a
.B duplicate-of
link to it.
.TP
.B reopen
Set a closed issue's status back to open. An earlier close reason is left as it
is; it only resolves while the status is terminal.
.TP
.B remove
Unlist an issue, or retract one comment on it. Nothing is erased.
.TP
.B log
The tracker's own history, one commit per change. Accepts
.BR git-log (1)
options.
.TP
.B pull
Import issues from another clone or from a bridge. The target is
.RI [ scheme :] remote :
a bare remote is a git fetch,
.B github:
reads GitHub's API,
.B ado:
reads Azure DevOps', and
.B gitea:
reads a Gitea or Forgejo repository's. An
.B ado:
target may name an area after a
.BR # ,
as in
.BR ado:origin#Web/Auth ,
which scopes the import to that area and everything under it.
.TP
.B push
Send issues to another clone or to a bridge. Takes the same targets. An area on
an
.B ado:
target says where a work item this push
.I creates
is filed; one that already exists always keeps the area it has.
.PP
Ids abbreviate like object names, and an ambiguous prefix is refused rather
than resolved arbitrarily. A comment's prefix only has to be unique within its
issue.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B -h
Print the command list. Note that
.B git issue --help
opens this manual page, because
.BR git (1)
rewrites
.B --help
into
.BR "git help issue" ;
run
.B git-issue --help
to get the short command list instead.
.PP
Every command takes its own
.BR --help ,
which prints that command's flags:
.BR "git issue list --help" .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
File an issue, then list what is open:
.PP
.RS
.EX
git issue add -t "Anchor blobs are pruned by gc" -d "Seen on a fresh clone."
git issue --state open
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show one issue, comment on it, and share it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
git issue 4b0755a3e769
git issue comment 4b0755a3e769 -m "Confirmed."
git issue push origin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Import from Azure DevOps, picking an area the first time, and write a change
back:
.PP
.RS
.EX
git issue pull ado:origin
git issue edit 4b0755a3e769
git issue push ado:origin
.EE
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GITHUB_TOKEN
Token for the GitHub bridge; then
.BR GH_TOKEN ,
then
.BR "gh auth token" ,
then git's credential helpers.
.TP
.B AZURE_DEVOPS_EXT_PAT
Personal access token for the Azure DevOps bridge; then
.BR AZURE_DEVOPS_PAT ,
then
.BR SYSTEM_ACCESSTOKEN ,
then
.BR "az account get-access-token" ,
then git's credential helpers.
.TP
.B GITEA_TOKEN
Token for the Gitea bridge; then
.BR FORGEJO_TOKEN ,
then git's credential helpers.
.TP
.B GIT_PAGER
Pager for output longer than a screen; then
.BR core.pager ,
then
.BR PAGER ,
then
.BR less .
Set any of them to
.B cat
to page nothing.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Print plain, greppable output with no colour and no truncation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I docs/
The specification:
.I storage-model.md
for where bytes live,
.I blob-format.md
for what is inside one entity,
.I issues.md
for the issue vocabulary,
.I bridge-github.md
and
.I bridge-ado.md
for what each bridge maps onto it.
.TP
.I .git/config
.B issue.<tracker>.area
holds the Azure DevOps area a pull is scoped to, chosen once per clone.
.TP
.I .git/git-issue/sync.json
How far each tracker has been read. Local and disposable; losing it costs one
full re-import.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-notes (1),
.BR git-log (1)
